{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a251c730\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nStamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).\n- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.\n- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.\n- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.\n\nKey insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f3a5a89\" lines=\"22-36\">\n<description>\nPaint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\n- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\n- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\n- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\n- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\n\nKey insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\nKey insight: The connector pixels define the unique docking point and direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n<description>\nTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n\nRule:\n- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n\nKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\" lines=\"120-136\">\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"aa4ec2a5\" lines=\"52-66\">\n<description>\nAdd red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.\n\nRule:\n- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).\n- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).\n- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.\n\nKey insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects\nthat actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f215267\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nCount same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\n- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all small patterns from the grid.\n- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\n- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\n\nKey insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \"absorbed\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"eee78d87\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored 3x3 contour motif into a 16x16 periodic lattice with the motif stamped in maroon at the center.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is 6x6 with orange background and one non-orange color.\n- The non-orange pixels have a tight 3x3 bbox containing one contour glyph.\n- The glyph is one of the task's period-3 contour forms: plus, H, X, or four corners.\n\nRule:\n- Decode the 3x3 glyph into the period-3 orange phase tile.\n- Fill a black 16x16 canvas by repeating that orange phase tile.\n- Tile the original 3x3 glyph 2x2 and recolor its true pixels to maroon in the centered 6x6 region.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored object is not copied at its input location; it is a small contour code whose phase tells where orange appears in the repeated lattice.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
